<!--
  ambit D10 and D8 — cross-agent fingerprint propagation, and poisoned instruction files.

  Manager-side. IDs 100280-100299. Allocation map in ambit_mcp_rules.xml.

  D10 IS DELIBERATELY PARTIAL, and docs/03-detection.md's investigation says why. The
  detector asks whether the set of fingerprints an agent ingested intersects the set
  another agent later emitted. A Wazuh rule cannot express a set intersection: same_field
  is scalar equality, and the JSON decoder cannot carry an array of objects. So ambitd
  pre-selects at most one high-specificity fingerprint per event as the scalar
  prov_fp_notable, and the rules below are a tripwire on that scalar. The real detector is
  a scheduled job over the feature columns, not a rule, and it lands in M5.

  What the tripwire asserts, and what it does not:

  - 100281 means the same fingerprint was seen in two different sessions inside an hour.
    "Different session" is expressed with not_same_field on session_id next to same_field on
    prov_fp_notable. Both were accepted by wazuh-analysisd -t and behaved as intended under
    wazuh-logtest on 4.14.8 (observed offline): one session repeating a fingerprint raises
    nothing, a second session raising it does. The 3600s and 7200s windows themselves were
    not exercised, because logtest replays instantly.
  - 100282 counts 100281 alerts, not raw sightings. It fires on the fifth sighting across
    sessions: by then 100281's conditions have matched four times counting that event, and
    that event alerts as 100282. It cannot count 100280 events directly: once 100281 alerts
    on an event, that event is no longer a 100280 match, so a rule counting 100280 would
    never see it.
  - Wazuh counts an event toward if_matched_sid only if that rule was the one that alerted on
    it. An event that carries a fingerprint and also matches a higher-level rule (100302,
    secret material in a network command, is the one measured) alerts as that rule and is
    never counted here. D10 therefore undercounts exactly when something more serious already
    alerted on the same event.
  - Direction is lost. D10 means "ingest in X, output in Y", and the rule sees sightings of a
    value without ordering them. ambitd emits prov_fp_role "ingest" only, so there is no
    direction to recover from the event either.
  - Repeats inside one session are suppressed at the source: ambitd emits prov_fp_notable
    only the first time a session sees a fingerprint, so a session re-reading the same
    content adds no further sightings. Its per-session memory is bounded, so a full set can
    cause a repeat sighting but never a missed one, and it resets when ambitd restarts;
    not_same_field is what covers that case.
-->

<group name="ambit,ambit_provenance,">

  <!-- ===================== D10 — propagation tripwire ===================== -->

  <!--
    Base: an event carrying a notable fingerprint. Level 1, not 0: if_matched_sid counts only
    events whose final alert was exactly the named rule, and a level 0 match is not counted,
    so at level 0 the two rules below could never fire (observed offline). Level 1 is below
    Wazuh's default log alert level of 3, so these events are counted without alerting
    (alert=no under logtest). A manager configured with a lower log_alert_level would write
    each one out; that setting was not tested.
  -->
  <rule id="100280" level="1">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="prov_fp_notable">\.+</field>
    <description>ambit: event carries a notable fingerprint ($(prov_fp_role))</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ambit_d10,runbook_D10,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    The tripwire: the same high-specificity fingerprint seen in two different sessions within
    an hour. Read the caveats in the file header before treating this as propagation: a shared
    internal domain in two agents' tool results is what a company wiki looks like.

    No ignore: ignore silences the rule for every fingerprint, not per same_field value, so
    one fingerprint's alert would hide every other fingerprint's for its whole duration.
  -->
  <rule id="100281" level="10" frequency="2" timeframe="3600">
    <if_matched_sid>100280</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>prov_fp_notable</same_field>
    <not_same_field>session_id</not_same_field>
    <description>ambit D10: fingerprint $(prov_fp_notable) seen in two sessions within 3600s - possible cross-agent propagation</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ambit_d10,runbook_D10,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    The same fingerprint across several sessions is a stronger signal than two, and cheap to
    add: propagation between several agents produces more than one pair. Higher level, same
    caveats. It counts 100281 matches (see the file header for why it cannot count 100280): the
    fourth within two hours, counting the current event, is the fifth sighting across sessions.
  -->
  <rule id="100282" level="12" frequency="4" timeframe="7200">
    <if_matched_sid>100281</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>prov_fp_notable</same_field>
    <description>ambit D10: fingerprint $(prov_fp_notable) seen 5+ times across sessions within 7200s - propagation candidate</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1105</id>
      <id>T1570</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ambit_d10,runbook_D10,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    A provenance edge is ambitd's own statement that this action carried a value which
    also appeared in untrusted content it ingested earlier in the same session — the
    signal D10's tripwire only approximates. The engine is internal/prov; an edge is
    evidence for a reviewer, not proof of copying, and the model re-wording the value
    defeats it entirely. Only the strongest edge and the count reach this rule; the
    raw edges and the ingest they point at are in the local spool.

    prov_edge_confidence separates a spelled-out URL or a checksum-valid IBAN (0.95), an
    email or opaque token (0.90) from a bare domain (0.80), and a domain the operator trusts for content
    (0.40). A trusted domain is down-weighted rather than dropped because a trusted
    code host is also an exfiltration sink. The rule does not threshold on it yet: the
    first deployment is meant to show where that line belongs.
  -->
  <rule id="100283" level="12">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="prov_edge_count">\.+</field>
    <description>ambit: action linked to untrusted ingest - $(prov_edge_count) edge(s), strongest $(prov_edge_class) at $(prov_edge_confidence), from $(prov_edge_from)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ambit_provenance,runbook_D10,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================== D8 — poisoned instruction file ===================== -->

  <!--
    D8 proper: Claude Code loaded instructions from a path nobody has classified as
    trusted. This is the repo-carried injection route — a CLAUDE.md in a cloned
    repository, or a .claude/rules file arriving with a dependency — and
    InstructionsLoaded is the only event that reports it.

    config_trusted is computed on the endpoint against the operator's trusted-repo list,
    so false means "not on the list", not "known bad". The zone label says where it came
    from and is cleartext; the path is a keyed digest.
  -->
  <rule id="100290" level="10">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="kind">^instructions_loaded$</field>
    <field name="config_trusted">^false$</field>
    <description>ambit D8: instruction file loaded from an untrusted path - zone $(config_zone), reason $(config_load_reason) on $(endpoint_id)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1195.002</id>
      <id>T1546</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ambit_d8,runbook_D8,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    The same, from a zone that is untrusted in its own right: a dependency directory, a
    downloads folder, anything git reports as recently created and untracked. An
    instruction file there was not written by the developer.
  -->
  <rule id="100291" level="13">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="kind">^instructions_loaded$</field>
    <field name="config_trusted">^false$</field>
    <field name="config_zone">^untrusted$</field>
    <description>ambit D8: instruction file loaded from an UNTRUSTED ZONE on $(endpoint_id) - dependency-carried or downloaded instructions</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1195.002</id>
      <id>T1546</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ambit_d8,runbook_D8,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Several untrusted instruction files in one session. A single one is often a developer
    working in a repo nobody has added to the trusted list; a handful in minutes is a
    session pulling in instructions from everywhere it can reach.
  -->
  <rule id="100292" level="12" frequency="3" timeframe="600">
    <if_matched_sid>100290</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>session_id</same_field>
    <description>ambit D8: 3+ untrusted instruction files loaded in one session within 600s</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1195.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ambit_d8,runbook_D8,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
